  "common_pitfalls": [
    "Using LLMs for variant correctness verification creates circular dependencies: RIDE relies on GPT-5-mini to verify generated questions, GSM-Plus uses GPT-4 with 18.85% human revision rate, and GSM-Symbolic's statistical claims were challenged at ICLR for methodological issues. The CRADE design correctly avoids this by requiring SymPy-backed symbolic verification, but the temptation to 'just use an LLM' for edge cases will be strong — resist it, because reviewers will immediately flag the circularity.",
    "Claiming contamination effects without controlled experiments: 'Soft Contamination' succeeded because it trained models from scratch with deliberate contamination insertion; 'How Much Can We Forget' similarly used controlled experiments. Putnam-AXIOM documents a 41.9%→22.3% drop but explicitly acknowledges it cannot decompose the drop into memorization vs. brittleness vs. distributional shift. Reviewers will ask 'how do you know this is contamination and not just distributional shift?' — CRADE must have a falsifiable answer.",
    "Weak statistical methodology undermining correct intuitions: GSM-Symbolic's core insight (LLM math is fragile) is largely correct, but the ICLR blog critique showed their one-sample t-test was inappropriate and only 3/25 models showed individually significant drops. metabench explicitly discusses the violated IRT independence assumption. JECS provides asymptotic but not finite-sample guarantees. Use appropriate statistical tests, report confidence intervals, and address multiple comparisons — reviewers in this area are statistically sophisticated.",
    "Building 'yet another benchmark' without methodological contribution: GSM-Plus, GSM-Symbolic, MATH-Perturb, Putnam-AXIOM, LiveBench, and MathArena already cover the perturbation-benchmark space thoroughly. Papers that only construct a new perturbed dataset without a novel evaluation methodology, theoretical framework, or diagnostic decomposition will be rejected as incremental. CRADE must be framed as a framework/methodology applicable to ANY benchmark, not as a new dataset.",
    "Ignoring the scalability paradox for frontier models: FaithCoT-Bench shows detection methods degrade on advanced models (stronger models produce more deceptive CoTs). 'On The Fragility of Benchmark Contamination Detection' (seed) shows detection fails for reasoning models. 'Does RL Really Incentivize Reasoning' only validated up to 32B parameters. Any method that works only on smaller/weaker models will face 'does this generalize to frontier?' at review. CRADE must explicitly evaluate on reasoning models (o1, R1, QwQ) to avoid this critique.",
    "Presenting a monolithic framework without ablation: Reviewers consistently reward component-level analysis (RIDE ablates IRT reward vs. correctness reward; GenPRM ablates RPE vs. binary labels). A framework paper like CRADE that combines contamination detection + variant generation + step-level evaluation + IRT scoring MUST ablate each component independently, showing what each adds. 'The whole is greater than the sum' claims require proof, not assertion.",
    "Conflating IRT model fit with genuine construct validity: metabench's single-factor structure may reflect homogeneity of Open LLM Leaderboard submissions rather than true unidimensionality. tinyBenchmarks' λ-weighting assumes the IRT model generalizes to unseen LLMs. CRADE's 3PL with contamination indicators adds parameters, increasing overfitting risk on smaller response matrices. Report model fit statistics (RMSEA, CFI), use cross-validation, and test whether the contamination-aware model actually predicts held-out performance better than the simpler alternative.",
    "Underestimating the MathArena ground-truth validation complexity: The constraint to validate against MathArena's uncontaminated 2025 competition data is smart but introduces a dependency on MathArena's own contamination assumptions. If any evaluated model has seen paraphrases of competition problems (per 'Soft Contamination's findings that semantic duplicates escape standard decontamination), the 'ground truth' is itself compromised. Plan a robustness check of the ground truth, not just validation against it."
  ],

  "field_trajectory": "The field is converging on three macro-directions that will define the next 2-3 years of LLM evaluation research. First, the integration of psychometric measurement theory with contamination-aware evaluation is inevitable — every IRT paper (metabench, tinyBenchmarks, ATLAS, RIDE) explicitly acknowledges contamination as their blind spot, and every contamination paper (CoDeC, JECS, 'Soft Contamination') lacks principled scoring frameworks. The first paper to close this loop with joint estimation will define the subfield, and the 2025-2026 window is narrow before multiple groups converge on this idea. Second, step-level and process-level evaluation is becoming mandatory rather than optional — ProcessBench's finding that 51.8% of correct-answer solutions contain process errors, GenPRM's acceptance at AAAI 2026, and ReasonEval's acceptance at AAAI 2025 all signal that answer-only evaluation is no longer sufficient for top venues. The next frontier is adversarial step-level evaluation: not just 'is this step correct?' but 'does this step change appropriately when the problem changes?' — which nobody has measured. Third, the 'Does RL Really Incentivize Reasoning' finding (NeurIPS 2025 Best Paper Runner-Up) is reshaping how the field thinks about what LLM evaluation actually measures. If RLVR only redistributes probability mass without expanding reasoning capacity, then contamination detection and robustness testing are measuring properties of the pre-training distribution, not the alignment procedure — meaning CRADE's decomposition into genuine ability + contamination boost − robustness deficit is measuring something more fundamental than previously appreciated. The implication is that the field will move from 'does this model score well?' toward 'what latent capability does this model actually have, independent of contamination, fine-tuning artifacts, and sampling strategy?' — and psychometric frameworks like IRT, extended for the LLM setting, are the natural language for answering that question.",

  "meta_advice": "If you're writing a strong 2026 paper in this area, here is what the evidence says. First, occupy an intersection that nobody else sits at — the papers with the highest impact (RIDE at the IRT×perturbation intersection, GenPRM at the generation×verification intersection, 'Does RL Really Incentivize Reasoning' at the RL×evaluation intersection) all combine ideas from communities that don't talk to each other. The contamination-detection community (CoDeC, JECS, DCR), the adversarial-robustness community (GSM-Plus, MaSTer, MATH-Perturb), and the IRT-for-LLMs community (metabench, tinyBenchmarks, ATLAS) cite almost none of the same papers — sitting at their three-way intersection gives you a structural advantage. Second, anchor your contribution in formal theory, not just empirical findings — the contamination-aware 3PL IRT decomposition (Observed = G + C − R) is the right level of formalism: concrete enough to implement, general enough to be a contribution beyond any single benchmark, and grounded in a 70-year measurement theory tradition that reviewers respect. Third, avoid the 'framework paper' trap where you present many components but each is shallow. Reviewers will evaluate each module against its best standalone competitor: your contamination detection against CoDeC (99.9% AUC), your variant generation against MaSTer (48% accuracy reduction), your step-level evaluation against GenPRM (87.9 F1). You cannot beat each specialist — instead, show that the joint modeling produces insights impossible from any component alone. The ablation structure should be: full CRADE vs. detection-only vs. variant-only vs. IRT-without-contamination, demonstrating that the decomposition reveals genuine capability scores that better predict performance on truly uncontaminated data (MathArena) than any single-component alternative. Fourth, evaluate on reasoning models (o1, R1, QwQ) or face the 'scalability paradox' critique — FaithCoT-Bench and Kocetkov et al. both show that methods developed on standard models fail on reasoning models. Fifth, release everything: the contamination-aware IRT implementation, the variant generation pipeline, the SPA metric code, and the calibrated scores for evaluated models. In 2025-2026, papers without open artifacts face an increasingly steep acceptance penalty, and your artifacts become the infrastructure that makes future work cite you. Finally, manage the 8-10 week timeline ruthlessly: the SymPy variant generation and IRT fitting are engineering-heavy and should start immediately, the multi-model evaluation matrix is the compute bottleneck (~120 H100-hours), and the MathArena validation is the credibility linchpin — if calibrated scores don't predict MathArena performance better than raw scores, the paper's core claim fails. Run the MathArena validation early enough to pivot if needed."
}
